{"duration_api_ms":110091,"stop_reason":"end_turn","session_id":"f53c7c74-5a2c-4671-8c6f-d992741ec221","total_cost_usd":0.35296340000000004,"usage":{"input_tokens":24,"cache_creation_input_tokens":17390,"cache_read_input_tokens":305787,"output_tokens":10238,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":6315},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":17390},"inference_geo":"global","iterations":[],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"modelUsage":{"claude-opus-5-5":{"inputTokens":24,"outputTokens":10238,"cacheReadInputTokens":305787,"cacheCreationInputTokens":17390,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.35296340000000004,"contextWindow":1000000,"maxOutputTokens":128000,"thinkingTokens":6315,"canonicalModel":"claude-opus-5-5","provider":"firstParty","costBasis":"list"}},"permission_denials":[],"terminal_reason":"completed","fast_mode_state":"off","fast_mode_disabled_reason":"sdk_opt_in_required","subagent_stats":{"spawned":0,"requested":{"background":0,"foreground":0,"unset":0},"started_in_background":0,"max_depth":0,"spawned_by_subagents":0,"completed":0,"failed":0,"killed":{"parent":0,"user":0,"system":0},"refused":{"depth_limit":0,"concurrency_limit":0,"budget":0},"by_type":{}},"safety_stops":0,"is_error":false,"num_turns":12,"subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"The answer is in `/work/answer.md`. sol wrote it; I checked the proof step by step and against exact computations, and signed off as FINAL on the board.\n\n**Condition** (with V = v_A + v_B + v_C and all three single-item values positive): the Vickrey-nearest point equals the centroid of F exactly when one of these holds:\n1. **v_P ≤ min(v_A, v_B, v_C).** All VCG payments are 0, the price caps never bind, and F is the full equilateral triangle Σp = v_P. The common point is (v_P/3, v_P/3, v_P/3).\n2. **v_P ≥ V − min(v_i)**, meaning v_P is at least the sum of every pair of single-item values. Every VCG payment is at least 0 by its formula, no cap binds, and F is an equilateral triangle again. The common point is p_i = v_i − (V − v_P)/3.\n3. **v_A = v_B = v_C.** Symmetry forces both points to (v_P/3, v_P/3, v_P/3).\n\nIf any single-item value is zero, the two points always coincide, because F is then a segment or a point.\n\n**How the proof goes:** In the core, every coalition constraint reduces to \"total payment ≥ v_P\", so R = v_P. Shifting by the VCG payments turns F into a triangle with corners cut off by caps. The Vickrey-nearest point is then the point of that shape closest to the origin. If it equals the centroid, the point lies strictly inside, so all its shifted coordinates are equal. When the caps differ and actually cut the triangle, reflecting across the swap of the smallest-capped coordinate with a larger one shows the centroid's coordinates are strictly unequal. Equality is therefore impossible outside the three cases.\n\n**Example where they differ:** (v_A, v_B, v_C) = (1, 2, 3) and v_P = 3. VCG charges (0, 0, 0) and F is a parallelogram. Its centroid is (1/2, 1, 3/2), but the Vickrey-nearest point is (1, 1, 1).\n\n**Checks I ran:**\n- Exact-fraction brute force over about 19,000 random instances with values 1–12: the condition matched in every case.\n- Every case with a zero value among 0–8: the points always coincided.\n- The example: confirmed exactly.\n\nMy first guess had missed case 1; sol caught it and the numbers confirmed it.","ttft_ms":2306,"type":"result","duration_ms":198799,"uuid":"7c4a415a-f676-4960-a6e8-60d07eb76fcf","ttft_stream_ms":1453,"time_to_request_ms":54,"first_content_frame_ms":1453,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0}
